WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:02.697
Government is not a monolithic&nbsp;
thing, which is your enemy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.697 --> 00:00:07.723
It's a multitudinous thing, which is your enemy.

00:00:07.723 --> 00:00:11.440
Our dream was to enable the&nbsp;
future of human freedom.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:16.043
In the early 1990s, a group of&nbsp;mathematicians, misfits, hackers,&nbsp;and hobbyists 

00:00:16.043 --> 00:00:20.925
came together around a&nbsp;shared belief that the internet would either demolish artificial walls

00:00:20.925 --> 00:00:23.585
or lay the groundwork for an Orwellian state.

00:00:23.585 --> 00:00:27.434
We can prevent the Ministry of Truth.

00:00:27.434 --> 00:00:30.080
They saw a branch of&nbsp;mathematics called cryptography&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:34.080
as a weapon against central planning and&nbsp;surveillance in this new virtual world.

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:37.634
And what is a cypherpunk? Cypherpunks are cryptography activists.

00:00:37.634 --> 00:00:42.820
The more I realize how it was a&nbsp;
really pivotal technology for society.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.752
The National Security Agency&nbsp;
declared the unrestrained public&nbsp;discussion 

00:00:46.752 --> 00:00:50.490
of cryptographic research&nbsp;
threatened our national security.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:55.341
I saw this as the critical fork in the&nbsp;
road for the future of human freedom.

00:00:55.341 --> 00:00:58.880
Coming up, Reason presents a four-part&nbsp;
series on a movement that foresaw both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.560
the promise and the peril that lay ahead&nbsp;
when the internet upended the world.

00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.000
It's like standing on top of the mountain&nbsp;
and seeing that this is out there.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.840
You people who are here can say, "Okay, what kind of world do we really&nbsp;want to build?" And build it. 

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:27.808
Imagine, if you will, sitting down to&nbsp;your morning coffee, turning on your&nbsp;home computer to read the day's newspaper. 

00:01:27.808 --> 00:01:30.400
Well, it's not as far-fetched as it may seem.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:33.200
The freedom issues were very important to us.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:37.200
This is a sample of what you&nbsp;
can order through your computer.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.120
It combines the power of your computer&nbsp;
with the convenience of your telephone.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:50.080
We saw that information technology could create&nbsp;
a 1984 style tyrannical top-down control society.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:57.120
Or it could develop into something that is&nbsp;
a decentralized enabler of human freedom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:02.640
And even back then, our dream was to&nbsp;
enable the future of human freedom.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:08.955
In the late 1980s at the dawn of the online&nbsp;
age, a group of young computer scientists in&nbsp;Silicon Valley 

00:02:08.955 --> 00:02:12.800
started grappling with what&nbsp;
this new technology would mean for society.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:19.093
We took a look at the potential of computer&nbsp;
systems and saw that for everything about&nbsp;civilization 

00:02:19.093 --> 00:02:23.680
that enables it to work,&nbsp;
we can enable it to work much better.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:27.280
The most influential figure in what&nbsp;
would become the cypherpunk movement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:31.840
was the physicist, Tim May, who envisioned the&nbsp;
future internet as a place for what he would dub&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:35.760
"crypto-anarchy," where lawbreakers,&nbsp;
radicals, and political dissidents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:39.200
could shield their activities from&nbsp;
government surveillance and control.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:41.360
People doing things that&nbsp;
didn't require permissions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.605
from anyone because the people were&nbsp;
untraceable, it couldn't be stopped.

00:02:44.605 --> 00:02:48.240
But there was another group that saw the&nbsp;
potential of computers to remake society&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:53.440
gradually from within, by decentralizing commerce&nbsp;
and improving the dissemination of knowledge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:57.360
They were dubbed the High-Tech Hayekians&nbsp;
because their worldview was influenced by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.000
the work of Austrian economist, Friedrich Hayek.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.520
The fundamental insights of Hayekian economics was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:07.095
how separately formulated plans tend to&nbsp;
mesh well.

00:03:07.095 --> 00:03:18.320
And we saw electronic media as a enabler of coordination of a complexity that&nbsp;the world previously was really not capable of.

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:21.094
The clash of visions between the&nbsp;High-Tech Hayekians,

00:03:21.094 --> 00:03:24.211
who wanted to&nbsp;build a borderless, frictionless, global&nbsp;marketplace,

00:03:24.211 --> 00:03:29.418
and the crypto-anarchist who saw the internet as a technological&nbsp;means of undermining all governments,

00:03:29.418 --> 00:03:33.547
set the course for the cypherpunk movement.

00:03:33.547 --> 00:03:35.680
If May was the father of crypto-anarchy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:41.106
the central figure for The High-Tech Hayekians&nbsp;
was the economist and entrepreneur, Phil Salin.

00:03:41.106 --> 00:03:42.800
Now, if you own a personal computer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.286
we are told you can shop at home on&nbsp;
what's called The Electronic Mall.

00:03:46.286 --> 00:03:50.800
It was a few years before the invention of the World&nbsp;
Wide Web but computer companies were offering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:54.240
a new type of service in which customers&nbsp;
could connect through their phone lines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:58.160
to post messages to a bulletin board,&nbsp;
check their horoscopes, or go shopping.

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:00.000
You can buy items from department stores&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.378
ranging from Nordstroms to Neiman&nbsp;
Marcus, all via computer screen.

00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:08.480
Salin, whose outlook was shaped&nbsp;
by his careful reading of Hayek,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:12.400
understood that these services weren't&nbsp;
merely a curiosity or a digital version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:16.250
of the old mail order catalog, they were&nbsp;
the beginning of something that had the&nbsp;potential

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:22.039
to one day erase national boundaries&nbsp;
through trade and undermine government power.

00:04:22.039 --> 00:04:28.240
In a prescient 1991 essay, he foresaw&nbsp;
how the internet would change the world by 1995,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:32.480
2000, and beyond. The static&nbsp;
role of store and shopper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:38.320
seller and buyer, author and reader, systems&nbsp;
defined by their one-way information flows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:43.835
will be replaced by new forms of media&nbsp;
enabling two-way information flows.

00:04:43.835 --> 00:04:47.760
Before getting involved with computers,&nbsp;
Salin had run a private rocket company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:51.680
and had coauthored a congressional study&nbsp;
critical of NASA's wasteful spending.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:56.217
But in his view, there was continuity between&nbsp;
his interest in space travel and computing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.217 --> 00:05:00.536
because in both industries, technology's&nbsp;
purpose was to drive down the transaction&nbsp;costs

00:05:00.536 --> 00:05:04.591
that impede human activity,&nbsp;
making it feasible to explore new&nbsp;frontiers,

00:05:04.591 --> 00:05:08.694
and interact in ways that would&nbsp;
otherwise be prohibitively expensive.

00:05:08.694 --> 00:05:14.785
Now, thanks to breakthroughs in computing, the&nbsp;
ability to buy or obtain exactly the information&nbsp;you need

00:05:14.785 --> 00:05:21.955
when you want it, in the form you&nbsp;want it, is about to explode at a speed&nbsp;unmatched since the invention of printing.

00:05:21.955 --> 00:05:25.760
But&nbsp;Salin was foremost an entrepreneur, and in the mid-1980s,

00:05:25.760 --> 00:05:30.305
he set out to launch one of those&nbsp;platforms for buying and selling information.

00:05:30.305 --> 00:05:34.000
He called it the American&nbsp;
Information Exchange or AMIX.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:38.320
This business that we're doing is to&nbsp;
make possible a new kind of thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:42.800
which is being able to buy&nbsp;
and sell information online.

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:46.038
AMIX was a radical idea in the mid&nbsp;1980s.

00:05:46.038 --> 00:05:52.305
Users could buy or sell advice&nbsp;about the real estate market, writing&nbsp;software, or what companies to invest in.

00:05:52.305 --> 00:05:57.347
Now, you can say to somebody, so&nbsp;
it's like eBay, or like Priceline,&nbsp;or like Amazon.

00:05:57.347 --> 00:06:02.939
When we tried&nbsp;to explain this to people, they simply couldn't get it, they could not wrap their heads around this. 

00:06:02.939 --> 00:06:09.200
Some of them, I think, couldn't imagine the computational&nbsp;end of it. Milton Friedman didn't get it.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:13.862
Though it was similar in ways to the online&nbsp;
marketplaces that would launch a decade later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.862 --> 00:06:17.120
AMIX was more than just an idea before its time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.120 --> 00:06:20.320
What set it apart was it the&nbsp;
grounding in political philosophy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:25.280
and its lofty goal of elevating individual&nbsp;
decision-making over central planning.

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:33.138
It was an instantiation of Austrian&nbsp;
economics. Now put into code and put online. &nbsp;

00:06:33.138 --> 00:06:37.920
Something like this could have profound&nbsp;
human consequences. And that was the vision.

00:06:37.920 --> 00:06:43.040
"A fluid transaction-oriented market system,&nbsp;
with two-way feedback." Salin wrote, 

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:47.668
"would  result in crowding out monolithic,&nbsp;
mostly government bureaucracies."

00:06:47.668 --> 00:06:55.883
We were laying the philosophical foundations&nbsp;
of what is e-commerce, that you can now have creative explosion. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.883 --> 00:07:00.888
It's going to&nbsp;change the world. Yes, that's right.

00:07:00.888 --> 00:07:05.840
Intermediating among large populations&nbsp;
of human beings through electronic means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.840 --> 00:07:09.280
results in all kinds of fascinating&nbsp;
emergent social behavior.

00:07:09.280 --> 00:07:13.949
AMIX's chief architect was the pioneering software&nbsp;
engineer, Chip Morningstar,

00:07:13.949 --> 00:07:19.654
who had come from a stint at George Lucas's production studio, where&nbsp;he oversaw the creation of Habitat, 

00:07:19.654 --> 00:07:22.563
which was one of the first online virtual communities.

00:07:22.563 --> 00:07:25.474
After joining AMIX, Morningstar coauthored an essay

00:07:25.474 --> 00:07:30.517
which argued that in building a world in&nbsp;software, detailed central planning is impossible.

00:07:30.517 --> 00:07:35.953
The world is just more complicated. People&nbsp;are more complicated, you cannot know&nbsp;what all their goals are. 

00:07:35.953 --> 00:07:40.793
What you can do is&nbsp;you can put them into an environment in which&nbsp;certain kinds of things are possible.

00:07:40.793 --> 00:07:45.632
What&nbsp;you can't do is tell people what they want.

00:07:45.632 --> 00:07:51.773
Phil Salin, Mark Miller, and Chip Morningstar's&nbsp;ideas about how a future internet could decentralize society

00:07:51.773 --> 00:07:55.738
inspired Tim May to&nbsp;first articulate a radically different vision.

00:07:55.738 --> 00:07:59.760
May, who would go on to become the most&nbsp;
influential figure in the cypherpunk movement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:04.000
was introduced to Salin by&nbsp;
Morningstar in December of 1987.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:07.578
Chip said, "There's this guy out in&nbsp;
Santa Cruz that you ought to talk to."

00:08:07.578 --> 00:08:13.723
As a scientist at Intel, May had solved an issue affecting the reliability of the company's memory chips,

00:08:13.723 --> 00:08:15.809
called the Alpha&nbsp;Particle problem.

00:08:15.809 --> 00:08:20.770
Rich on stock options, he'd been spending his days at the beach,&nbsp;reading science fiction novels, 

00:08:20.770 --> 00:08:27.002
thinking about how networked personal computers could turn 
some of their futuristic scenarios into reality.

00:08:27.002 --> 00:08:32.560
So I met with Phil in Redwood City and he&nbsp;
described how AMIX would work. And I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.560 --> 00:08:37.440
"People aren't going to be selling meaningless&nbsp;
stuff like surfboard recommendations."

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:41.680
In May's view, AMIX could be useful&nbsp;
as a tool for undermining state power,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:44.499
but only by shattering legal and social norms.

00:08:44.499 --> 00:08:49.582
And I said, "It's going to be corporate&nbsp;
information. It's going to be bomber plans&nbsp;for the B-1 bomber.

00:08:49.582 --> 00:08:52.080
Who wants to buy&nbsp;that? A competitor or a company. 

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:55.280
Who wants to sell that? Someone who&nbsp;
has access to that information.

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:59.200
It's two different types of libertarians.&nbsp;
He didn't understand the extent to which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:04.459
you could benefit the world by lowering&nbsp;
transaction costs for everybody.

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:21.840
Like Salin and The High-Tech Hayekians, May saw&nbsp;the personal computer as enabling technology&nbsp;&nbsp;for sweeping societal changes. 

00:09:21.840 --> 00:09:26.615
But he had a different vision of a virtual marketplace for any and all goods and services,

00:09:26.615 --> 00:09:32.086
which he dubbed Black Net,&nbsp;or a technological means of undermining all&nbsp;governments. 

00:09:32.086 --> 00:09:38.911
On AMIX user activity was out in the open, but Black Net would be impervious to government tracking and surveillance.

00:09:38.911 --> 00:09:42.371
And perhaps&nbsp;it would be a network with no owners at all.

00:09:42.371 --> 00:09:46.560
Many people's first response would be to say&nbsp;
that Black Net won't be allowed to happen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.560 --> 00:09:50.708
Perhaps, but technology would&nbsp;
probably make it inevitable.

00:09:50.708 --> 00:09:55.120
If you don't physically meet the person if&nbsp;you don't even know what continent&nbsp;they're on, you can't coerce them.

00:09:55.120 --> 00:10:02.000
Tim May is a smart guy. He was great for&nbsp;
honing our ideas and technologies against.

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:06.782
But I think him as the shock jock for cypherpunks.

00:10:06.782 --> 00:10:13.280
I want to live in the world that surrounds us rather than trying and construct one from scratch.

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:20.720
We saw this as emerging from inside the&nbsp;
world and carrying the world along with it.

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:25.168
In 1988, AMIX was acquired by the software&nbsp;giant, Autodesk.

00:10:25.168 --> 00:10:30.539
A growing scene of young technologists came together around Salin and The High-Tech Hayekians, 

00:10:30.539 --> 00:10:36.548
all working towards a common vision of enhancing human 
freedom through networked personal computing.

00:10:36.548 --> 00:10:43.460
It was this hot house of ideas&nbsp;
from which a worldview was brewing.

00:10:43.460 --> 00:10:49.492
Salin died of cancer in 1991. The same year that&nbsp;
the World Wide Web became publicly available.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.492 --> 00:10:55.964
But his intellectual contributions and&nbsp;conflict with May had laid the groundwork&nbsp;for what would come next for the cypherpunks,

00:10:55.964 --> 00:11:00.126
the long effort to build a self-sovereign and&nbsp;anonymous form of digital money,

00:11:00.126 --> 00:11:03.501
and the embrace&nbsp;of a branch of mathematics called cryptography,

00:11:03.501 --> 00:11:10.772
which they came to view as the key to&nbsp;
personal liberation on the internet.

